(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["chem-12-14"] = {"14.1":{"s":"Living things are built from a few kinds of large molecules: carbohydrates, proteins, nucleic acids and lipids. They form structures and take part in every life process. This chapter covers carbohydrates, proteins and nucleic acids."},"14.2":{"s":"Carbohydrates are polyhydroxy aldehydes or ketones, or compounds that give them on hydrolysis. Monosaccharides (glucose, fructose) cannot be broken down further. Disaccharides (sucrose, maltose, lactose) give two units. Polysaccharides (starch, cellulose, glycogen) give many. Glucose exists mainly in ring forms. Sugars that reduce Tollens' or Fehling's reagent are reducing sugars; sucrose is not.","w":["Glucose is an aldohexose and fructose a ketohexose; both have the formula C₆H₁₂O₆.","Sucrose is a non-reducing sugar: both its reducing groups are used up in the glycosidic link."]},"14.3":{"s":"Proteins are polymers of α-amino acids joined by peptide bonds (–CO–NH–). Amino acids exist as zwitterions. Protein structure has four levels: primary (the sequence), secondary (α-helix, β-sheet), tertiary (overall folding) and quaternary (several chains together). Heat or acid can unfold a protein (denaturation). Enzymes are proteins that act as specific catalysts.","w":["Amino acids are amphoteric: at the isoelectric point the zwitterion has no net charge."]},"14.4":{"s":"Nucleic acids, DNA and RNA, carry hereditary information. They are chains of nucleotides; each nucleotide is a sugar, a phosphate and a nitrogen base. DNA has deoxyribose and the bases A, G, C and T, arranged as a double helix with A–T and G–C pairs. RNA has ribose and uracil in place of thymine, and is usually single-stranded.","w":["DNA has deoxyribose and thymine; RNA has ribose and uracil.","Base pairs in DNA: A with T (two hydrogen bonds), G with C (three)."]}};
